<%= render 'shared/player_preconnect', url: @embed_url, source: @entry.resolved_source %>

<script nonce="<%= content_security_policy_nonce %>">
  // Same two guards the watch page keeps, and for the same reasons: the embed is a third
  // party's page and will open windows and navigate its parent given the chance.
  window.open = function () { return null; };

  window.addEventListener('beforeunload', function (e) {
    if (window.leavingOnPurpose) return;

    if (document.activeElement && document.activeElement.tagName === 'IFRAME') {
      e.preventDefault();
      e.returnValue = '';
      return '';
    }
  });
</script>

<%# The same three parts as the watch page, and the same reasons for the split (see
    entries/watch.html.erb): the screen is what goes fullscreen and must survive a change of
    channel, the frame stack holds the player, and the chrome is everything that describes
    what is on right now and is replaced wholesale when the channel changes.

    The ids are the watch page's ids on purpose. cinema-navigation finds its frame and its
    chrome by name, so changing channel here costs exactly what it costs there -- including
    the channel below being warmed in a second frame -- with no cable-specific copy of it. %>
<div class="cinema__screen"
     data-controller="cinema-fullscreen cinema-navigation cable-guide"
     <%# The clock asking to move on is answered the same way the up-next card is on the
         watch page: the navigation controller fetches the next page and pastes it in, so
         the player's frame and any fullscreen survive the change of programme. %>
     <%# The clock gives the warming its cue here: there is no up-next card on a cable
         channel, and what comes next is not at an address of its own -- it is this same
         channel, asked what will be on at the moment it changes. %>
     data-action="cable-clock:move@document->cinema-navigation#moveFromEvent
                  cable-clock:warm@document->cinema-navigation#warmNext
                  <%# The guide is not replaced by a move, so it has to be told about one:
                      its highlight follows the channel however the channel was changed. %>
                  cinema-navigation:moved@document->cable-guide#channelChanged
                  player-keys:fullscreen@document->cinema-fullscreen#toggle"
     data-cable-guide-url-value="<%= cable_guide_path %>"
     data-cable-guide-channel-value="<%= @channel.id %>"
     <%# The clock in the guide has to run in the zone the schedule was written in, not the
         viewer's. Every time on the grid is a cable time; a clock ticking in local time
         would disagree with the column right next to it for anybody not in that zone. %>
     data-cable-guide-zone-value="<%= CableSchedule.zone.tzinfo.name %>"
     data-cable-guide-open-class="cinema__screen--guide"
     data-cinema-fullscreen-idle-class="cinema__screen--idle"
     <%# Unlike the watch page, the chrome here goes away windowed as well. A cable channel
         is something playing in a room rather than a page being worked through, and its
         banner is a caption that has said its piece -- long enough to read what is on, and
         short enough that the picture is not permanently framed. %>
     data-cinema-fullscreen-always-value="true"
     data-cinema-fullscreen-idle-delay-value="4000"
     data-cinema-navigation-moving-class="cinema__screen--moving"
     data-cinema-navigation-preload-value="true"
     <%# What the entries sidebar's script writes on the watch page. Nothing writes it here,
         and the unset default is placed for a sidebar this page does not have. %>
     style="--watch-controls-right: 120px;">

  <div id="cinema-frames" class="cinema__frames">
    <%# No `allowfullscreen`: fullscreen belongs to the container, exactly as on the watch
        page. See cinema_fullscreen_controller.js. %>
    <iframe id="cinema"
            class="cinema__frame cinema__frame--live"
            title="<%= @entry.name %>"
            referrerpolicy="origin"
            allow="autoplay"
            src="<%= @embed_url.presence || 'about:blank' %>"></iframe>
  </div>

  <%# Closes the guide on the way, so this always leaves you looking at the picture full
      size. With the guide up it sits over the player's own fullscreen button, which is dead
      -- the frame has no fullscreen permission -- so the one that works is on top of the
      one that does not. %>
  <%# A transparent strip across the player's scrubber, so it cannot be dragged. Everything
      else of theirs is left reachable, menus included -- see _cable.scss for where the line
      is drawn and why. Nothing is bound to it: catching the drag is the whole of its job. %>
  <div class="cable-guard<%= ' cable-guard--whole' if @in_break %>" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <%# A break with nothing to show. Rendered for every break rather than only the ones that
      start out empty, because a reel can refuse at any point -- taken down, made private,
      embedding switched off -- and cable-filler uncovers this when it hears the player say
      so. Better than a dead frame either way, and it is what a channel with nothing to fill
      the gap would put up. %>
  <% if @in_break %>
    <div id="cableInterlude" class="cable-interlude" <%= 'hidden' if @embed_url.present? %>>
      <p class="cable-interlude__card">We&rsquo;ll be right back</p>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <button type="button" class="cinema__fullscreen" title="Fullscreen"
          data-action="click->cable-guide#close click->cinema-fullscreen#toggle">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-expand"></i>
  </button>

  <%# The guide, sitting exactly on the player's own play/pause button at the other end of
      its bar -- the same trick the fullscreen button above plays on theirs, and for a
      firmer reason. There is no pausing a cable channel, so their button is not merely
      redundant here, it is wrong; covering it puts something useful where the eye already
      goes for a control. With the screen rather than the chrome, because it is the same
      button whatever is playing and a change of channel should not replace it. %>
  <button type="button" class="cinema__guide-button" title="TV Guide" aria-label="TV Guide"
          data-action="click->cable-guide#toggle">
    <i class="fa-solid fa-table-list"></i>
  </button>

  <div id="cinema-chrome"
       <%# player-keys, but with its transport switched off, and no player-progress at all.
           A cable channel plays to a clock: pausing it does not pause the channel and
           seeking within it does not move the channel, so both only put this viewer out of
           step with the listing and with everyone else watching. What is left of the
           keyboard is fullscreen and mute, neither of which touches the film. %>
       <%# cable-filler only through a break, and only then: it is watching one particular
           embed for one particular refusal. %>
       <%# cable-hold puts the film back on when somebody stops it. There is no pausing a
           channel that plays to a clock -- the schedule does not wait, so a pause only puts
           this viewer out of step with the listing and with everyone else watching. Absent
           through a break, where the frame is YouTube's and nothing here can drive it. %>
       data-controller="player-keys cable-clock<%= @in_break ? ' cable-filler' : ' cable-hold' %>"
       <% unless @in_break %>
         data-cable-hold-frame-value="cinema"
         data-cable-hold-adapter-value="<%= @entry.resolved_source&.sync_adapter %>"
       <% end %>
       <% if @in_break %>
         data-cable-filler-frame-value="cinema"
         data-cable-filler-card-value="cableInterlude"
       <% end %>
       data-player-keys-transport-value="false"
       <%# Which adapter drives this page's player, for anything that needs to reach it --
           cinema-navigation reads it off the fetched page to know how to quieten the
           channel it is warming. Its own attribute rather than one controller's value,
           because it describes the player and not any one reader of it. %>
       <%# Nil through a commercial break: the frame is YouTube's then, not the film's
           provider, and nothing here can drive it. Saying so keeps every listener honest --
           and stops the channel below being warmed into a player nobody can quieten. %>
       data-player-adapter="<%= @in_break ? nil : @entry.resolved_source&.sync_adapter %>"
       data-player-keys-adapter-value="<%= @in_break ? nil : @entry.resolved_source&.sync_adapter %>"
       data-player-keys-frame-value="cinema"
       data-cable-clock-frame-value="cinema"
       data-cable-clock-adapter-value="<%= @in_break ? nil : @entry.resolved_source&.sync_adapter %>"
       <%# Where to go when this programme is over: the channel itself, which answers with
           whatever the schedule says is on by the time the request lands. %>
       <%# Which channel this is, on the one element a move replaces -- the guide reads it
           back to move its highlight, since nothing else tells it the dial has turned. %>
       data-cable-channel-id="<%= @channel.id %>"
       <%# This channel as it will be the moment it changes, for warming ahead of the change. %>
       data-cinema-next-url="<%= @next_url %>"
       data-cable-clock-url-value="<%= cable_channel_path(@channel) %>"
       <%# The next time this channel shows something different, which is not always the end
           of the slot: a programme with a break after it changes twice. %>
       data-cable-clock-ends-at-value="<%= @next_change_at.utc.iso8601 %>"
       <%# Where the schedule thinks the film is, for comparing against the length the
           player reports -- a file shorter than the catalogue claims cannot be spotted any
           other way, and the player answers such a request by starting over. %>
       data-cable-clock-programme-starts-at-value="<%= @slot.starts_at.utc.iso8601 %>"
       <%# What the catalogue claims, and where to correct it. Zero means it claims nothing
           and the schedule is guessing -- see EntriesController#runtime. Signed in only,
           because correcting the catalogue is a write and guests do not get one. %>
       data-cable-clock-runtime-value="<%= @entry.length.to_i * 60 %>"
       <% if user_signed_in? %>
         data-cable-clock-runtime-url-value="<%= runtime_entry_path(@entry) %>"
         data-cable-clock-token-value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>"
       <% end %>
       <% unless @in_break %>
         <%# Where to go when the film runs out before the clock says it should. Absent once
             the break has started -- there is nothing after the adverts but the next slot. %>
         data-cable-clock-filler-url-value="<%= cable_channel_path(@channel, filler: 1) %>"
       <% end %>>

    <%= render 'cable/hud', channel: @channel, entry: @entry, user: current_user,
                            number: @channel_number, nearby: @nearby, current_slot: @slot %>

    <%# Marking a reviewable channel's film watched opens this, exactly as on the watch
        page -- _completion_status points both at the same #reviewModal. %>
    <% if user_signed_in? %>
      <%= render 'entries/review_modal' %>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# The guide. Outside the chrome deliberately: changing channel replaces the chrome, and
      a listing that vanished every time you tuned from it would be no use as a listing. It
      stays up, the picture in its corner changes, and the highlight moves.

      Empty until it is opened. The grid is fetched then, because it is only ever wanted on
      purpose and because a grid built into the page would go stale while a film played. %>
  <div class="tvguide" data-cable-guide-target="panel" hidden>
    <div class="tvguide__head">
      <%# The player is not moved into here -- moving an iframe in the DOM reloads it, and
          the whole point is that what you were watching keeps playing. The frame stack is
          resized into this corner by the stylesheet instead; this is the hole it sits in. %>
      <div class="tvguide__preview"></div>

      <%# What the panel describes follows the pointer around the grid, and falls back to
          what is actually playing when the pointer stops or leaves. Both links lead out of
          cable: the channel to its own page, the programme to the ordinary watch player,
          where it plays from the beginning and counts towards what you have seen. Neither
          carries data-cinema-move, so they are ordinary navigations rather than a tune. %>
      <div class="tvguide__detail">
        <a class="tvguide__detail-channel" data-cable-guide-target="detailChannel" href="#"></a>
        <h2 class="tvguide__detail-title">
          <a data-cable-guide-target="detailTitle" href="#"></a>
        </h2>
        <p class="tvguide__detail-meta" data-cable-guide-target="detailMeta"></p>
        <p class="tvguide__detail-watched" data-cable-guide-target="detailWatched" hidden>
          <i class="fa-solid fa-eye"></i> Watched
        </p>
        <p class="tvguide__detail-plot" data-cable-guide-target="detailPlot"></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <button type="button" class="tvguide__hide" data-action="click->cable-guide#close">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-compress-alt"></i> Hide Guide
    </button>

    <div class="tvguide__body" data-cable-guide-target="body">
      <p class="tvguide__loading">Loading listings&hellip;</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
